1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,723
Welcome to Module 5.

2
00:00:03,073 --> 00:00:07,241
In Module 4 you built an
agent loop with two small tools.

3
00:00:07,591 --> 00:00:10,413
Now we go deeper
into the tools themselves.

4
00:00:10,763 --> 00:00:16,722
By the end of this module, you'll build
a personal productivity agent that manages

5
00:00:16,722 --> 00:00:22,680
real tasks and notes, checks every
input, stops slow tools, and asks you before it

6
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:23,915
deletes anything.

7
00:00:25,715 --> 00:00:30,317
In this lesson, you'll learn what
tool calling is, and why it exists.

8
00:00:30,917 --> 00:00:34,438
You'll follow one tool call
from the request to the result.

9
00:00:35,038 --> 00:00:37,872
You'll read every
part of a tool definition.

10
00:00:38,472 --> 00:00:42,020
And you'll write tool
descriptions that a model uses correctly.

11
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:47,653
Let's start with what a tool
is, using an everyday picture.

12
00:00:48,003 --> 00:00:49,882
Imagine you hire an assistant.

13
00:00:50,232 --> 00:00:55,081
They're smart, but they can't reach
into your calendar or your files directly.

14
00:00:55,431 --> 00:00:57,711
So you lend them a phone with a few apps.

15
00:00:58,061 --> 00:01:02,788
Each app has a clear label, saying
what it does, and a form to fill in.

16
00:01:03,388 --> 00:01:05,117
The model is the assistant.

17
00:01:05,467 --> 00:01:09,267
It decides which app to
open, and what to type in the form.

18
00:01:09,867 --> 00:01:15,509
A tool is one app: a name, a description
of what it does, and a form for its inputs.

19
00:01:16,109 --> 00:01:17,762
And your code is the phone.

20
00:01:18,112 --> 00:01:22,028
It's the part that actually
runs the app, and shows the result.

21
00:01:22,628 --> 00:01:24,715
So why does tool calling exist?

22
00:01:25,065 --> 00:01:29,002
Because a language model on
its own can only write text.

23
00:01:29,352 --> 00:01:33,283
It can't read your to-do list,
save a note, or check today's date.

24
00:01:33,633 --> 00:01:38,761
Tools let it ask your code to do those
things, safely, through doors you choose.

25
00:01:40,561 --> 00:01:42,025
Now how does it work?

26
00:01:42,375 --> 00:01:45,200
Let's follow one tool call, step by step.

27
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:46,698
Step one.

28
00:01:47,048 --> 00:01:52,952
Your code sends a request with two
things: the user's message, and the list of tools

29
00:01:52,952 --> 00:01:54,217
the model may use.

30
00:01:54,817 --> 00:01:55,739
Step two.

31
00:01:56,089 --> 00:01:58,409
The model decides a tool would help.

32
00:01:58,759 --> 00:02:04,581
Instead of a final answer, it replies
with a tool use block: the tool's name, the

33
00:02:04,581 --> 00:02:06,449
input it wants, and an ID.

34
00:02:06,799 --> 00:02:08,762
The stop reason is tool use.

35
00:02:09,362 --> 00:02:10,355
Step three.

36
00:02:10,705 --> 00:02:13,767
Your code runs the real
function with that input.

37
00:02:14,117 --> 00:02:18,157
This is important: the
model never runs anything itself.

38
00:02:18,507 --> 00:02:19,731
It only asks.

39
00:02:20,331 --> 00:02:21,283
Step four.

40
00:02:21,633 --> 00:02:27,507
Your code sends the output back as a
tool result, with the same ID, in the next

41
00:02:27,507 --> 00:02:28,102
request.

42
00:02:28,452 --> 00:02:33,157
Then the model continues, and
either asks for another tool, or answers.

43
00:02:34,957 --> 00:02:36,679
Let's see that in real code.

44
00:02:37,029 --> 00:02:40,758
The code is on the left, and
what it printed is on the right.

45
00:02:41,358 --> 00:02:44,281
A tool definition is a Python dictionary.

46
00:02:44,631 --> 00:02:48,048
First, a name, and a
description written for the model.

47
00:02:48,398 --> 00:02:52,410
The description is how the
model decides when to use this tool.

48
00:02:53,010 --> 00:02:54,557
Next, the input schema.

49
00:02:54,907 --> 00:02:58,732
It's a JSON Schema: a
standard way to describe data.

50
00:02:59,082 --> 00:03:04,664
Here the form has three fields: a
title, a due date that can be empty, and a

51
00:03:04,664 --> 00:03:07,748
priority that must be low, normal or high.

52
00:03:08,348 --> 00:03:09,546
And strict, true.

53
00:03:09,896 --> 00:03:15,460
With strict mode, the API guarantees
that the input the model sends matches this

54
00:03:15,460 --> 00:03:18,659
schema: the right
fields, and the right types.

55
00:03:19,259 --> 00:03:23,343
Then we pass the tool in the
tools list, next to the messages.

56
00:03:23,693 --> 00:03:29,729
The first output line is the offline
notice: as in every module, a scripted fake

57
00:03:29,729 --> 00:03:32,520
plays the model, so no key is needed.

58
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:35,083
The stop reason is tool use.

59
00:03:35,433 --> 00:03:36,937
The model didn't answer.

60
00:03:37,287 --> 00:03:39,539
It's asking our code to run a tool.

61
00:03:40,139 --> 00:03:46,169
And here's the tool use block: the name,
add task, and the input, title pay rent, no

62
00:03:46,169 --> 00:03:47,892
due date, priority high.

63
00:03:48,242 --> 00:03:50,631
Notice that nothing has been added yet.

64
00:03:50,981 --> 00:03:53,172
Our code would run the function next.

65
00:03:53,522 --> 00:03:55,180
We'll do that in lesson two.

66
00:03:56,980 --> 00:03:59,282
Let's put every part in one table.

67
00:03:59,632 --> 00:04:03,550
The name: short, unique, and
usually a verb, like add task.

68
00:04:04,150 --> 00:04:07,849
The description: when to use
the tool, and what it returns.

69
00:04:08,199 --> 00:04:12,449
This is the model's only guide,
so it's the most important part.

70
00:04:13,049 --> 00:04:17,363
The input schema: the fields,
their types, and the allowed values.

71
00:04:17,963 --> 00:04:21,956
And strict: when it's true,
the inputs always match the schema.

72
00:04:23,756 --> 00:04:25,856
Now, where do developers go wrong?

73
00:04:26,206 --> 00:04:28,031
Mostly in the description.

74
00:04:28,381 --> 00:04:34,187
A name like task, and a description like
task tool, tells the model almost nothing.

75
00:04:34,537 --> 00:04:35,819
Does it add a task?

76
00:04:36,169 --> 00:04:37,092
Delete one?

77
00:04:37,442 --> 00:04:38,348
List them?

78
00:04:38,948 --> 00:04:41,231
A clear description says three things.

79
00:04:41,581 --> 00:04:45,252
What the tool does: adds a
task and returns its number.

80
00:04:45,602 --> 00:04:49,918
When to use it: when the user
asks to remember or plan something.

81
00:04:50,268 --> 00:04:53,800
And any rules: the due
date must be today or later.

82
00:04:54,150 --> 00:04:57,662
Write it as if for a new
colleague who has never seen your app.

83
00:04:59,462 --> 00:05:01,032
Pause the video and try it.

84
00:05:01,382 --> 00:05:04,824
Here's a tool called notes,
with the description: notes.

85
00:05:05,174 --> 00:05:07,349
Rewrite the name and the description.

86
00:05:07,949 --> 00:05:12,874
Ask yourself: what does it do, when
should the model use it, and what does it

87
00:05:12,874 --> 00:05:13,322
return?

88
00:05:19,922 --> 00:05:22,561
One good answer: name it search notes.

89
00:05:22,911 --> 00:05:28,651
Description: searches the user's notes
for words in the title or text, and returns

90
00:05:28,651 --> 00:05:29,981
the matching notes.

91
00:05:30,331 --> 00:05:34,055
Use it when the user asks
about something they saved earlier.

92
00:05:35,855 --> 00:05:36,997
Interview corner.

93
00:05:37,347 --> 00:05:38,898
Here's a common question.

94
00:05:39,248 --> 00:05:41,413
Does the model run the tool itself?

95
00:05:44,013 --> 00:05:45,443
A good answer: no.

96
00:05:45,793 --> 00:05:51,375
The model only requests a call, by
returning a tool use block with a name and an

97
00:05:51,375 --> 00:05:51,794
input.

98
00:05:52,144 --> 00:05:57,907
Your code runs the function, and sends
the output back as a tool result with the

99
00:05:57,907 --> 00:05:58,484
same ID.

100
00:05:58,834 --> 00:06:01,966
The trap is saying the
model executes your function.

101
00:06:02,316 --> 00:06:04,116
It never touches your code.

102
00:06:04,466 --> 00:06:06,186
You decide whether to run it.

103
00:06:06,786 --> 00:06:10,065
And a follow-up: what
makes a good tool definition?

104
00:06:12,665 --> 00:06:18,378
A clear verb for the name, a description
that says what the tool does, when to use

105
00:06:18,378 --> 00:06:24,231
it, and what it returns, and a tight
schema, with enums, required fields, and strict

106
00:06:24,231 --> 00:06:24,579
mode.

107
00:06:24,929 --> 00:06:27,374
The trap: focusing only on the schema.

108
00:06:27,724 --> 00:06:31,397
The description is what the
model reads to choose the tool.

109
00:06:33,197 --> 00:06:34,056
Quick check.

110
00:06:34,406 --> 00:06:36,369
The stop reason is tool use.

111
00:06:36,719 --> 00:06:38,107
What should your code do?

112
00:06:44,307 --> 00:06:50,131
Run the requested tool, and send the
output back as a tool result with the same ID.

113
00:06:51,931 --> 00:06:53,020
Let's recap.

114
00:06:53,370 --> 00:06:56,724
Tools let a model ask
your code to act in the world.

115
00:06:57,324 --> 00:07:03,024
The model requests, your code runs the
function, and the result goes back with the

116
00:07:03,024 --> 00:07:03,580
same ID.

117
00:07:04,180 --> 00:07:09,240
A definition has a name, a
description, an input schema, and strict mode.

118
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:14,906
And the description is the model's
guide: what it does, when to use it, what it

119
00:07:14,906 --> 00:07:16,381
returns, and the rules.

120
00:07:16,981 --> 00:07:19,770
Next lesson: many tools in one place.

121
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:25,860
You'll build a tool registry, generate
schemas from Pydantic, and handle several

122
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:27,869
tool calls in a single turn.

123
00:07:29,668 --> 00:07:32,076
One tool is easy to write by hand.

124
00:07:32,426 --> 00:07:38,057
Six tools, each with a schema, a
description, a permission and a timeout, is where

125
00:07:38,057 --> 00:07:40,872
hand-written dictionaries start to break.

126
00:07:41,222 --> 00:07:46,909
In this lesson, we'll organise many
tools in one place, let Pydantic write the

127
00:07:46,909 --> 00:07:52,086
schemas for us, and handle a model
that asks for several tools at once.

128
00:07:53,886 --> 00:07:56,435
You'll register tools with a decorator.

129
00:07:57,035 --> 00:08:00,967
Generate strict JSON
schemas from Pydantic classes.

130
00:08:01,567 --> 00:08:03,710
Dispatch a tool call by its name.

131
00:08:04,310 --> 00:08:07,070
And handle several tool calls in one turn.

132
00:08:08,870 --> 00:08:10,631
First, what is a registry?

133
00:08:10,981 --> 00:08:12,751
Think of a restaurant menu.

134
00:08:13,101 --> 00:08:17,360
Every dish is listed once, with
its name, description and price.

135
00:08:17,710 --> 00:08:21,226
When an order comes in, the
kitchen looks it up on the menu.

136
00:08:21,576 --> 00:08:24,780
A tool registry is
the menu for your agent.

137
00:08:25,130 --> 00:08:30,451
Every tool is listed once, with its
name, description, input form, permission, and

138
00:08:30,451 --> 00:08:30,970
timeout.

139
00:08:31,320 --> 00:08:34,937
When the model asks for a
tool, your code looks it up there.

140
00:08:35,537 --> 00:08:36,470
Why bother?

141
00:08:36,820 --> 00:08:39,359
Because one list
means one source of truth.

142
00:08:39,709 --> 00:08:45,143
The definitions you send to the model,
and the functions your code runs, can never

143
00:08:45,143 --> 00:08:45,938
drift apart.

144
00:08:47,738 --> 00:08:49,562
Here's how the project does it.

145
00:08:49,912 --> 00:08:55,542
A Tool is a frozen dataclass that
holds everything about one tool: its name,

146
00:08:55,542 --> 00:09:01,469
description, the Pydantic class for its
input, the function, its permission, its

147
00:09:01,469 --> 00:09:04,655
timeout, and an
optional confirmation text.

148
00:09:05,005 --> 00:09:07,267
We'll use permission in lesson five.

149
00:09:07,867 --> 00:09:12,985
The registry is just a dictionary,
from the tool's name to its Tool object.

150
00:09:13,585 --> 00:09:15,473
And tool is a decorator.

151
00:09:15,823 --> 00:09:19,201
A decorator is a function
that wraps another function.

152
00:09:19,551 --> 00:09:22,136
This one doesn't
change the function at all.

153
00:09:22,486 --> 00:09:27,627
It just adds it to the registry, using
the function's own name as the tool name.

154
00:09:29,427 --> 00:09:30,832
Now one real tool.

155
00:09:31,182 --> 00:09:33,850
First, the input as a Pydantic class.

156
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:37,955
A title between one and a
hundred and twenty characters.

157
00:09:38,305 --> 00:09:39,715
A due date, or None.

158
00:09:40,065 --> 00:09:43,254
And a priority that
must be low, normal, or high.

159
00:09:43,854 --> 00:09:49,001
Then the decorator, with the
description, the input class, and the permission:

160
00:09:49,001 --> 00:09:51,772
write, because adding a task changes data.

161
00:09:52,372 --> 00:09:53,840
And the function itself.

162
00:09:54,190 --> 00:09:59,787
Notice that it receives an AddTask
object, already checked, not a raw dictionary.

163
00:10:00,137 --> 00:10:06,307
So inside the function, a dot due really
is a date, and a dot priority really is one

164
00:10:06,307 --> 00:10:07,776
of the three values.

165
00:10:09,576 --> 00:10:14,045
Writing JSON schemas by hand
is slow, and easy to get wrong.

166
00:10:14,395 --> 00:10:16,362
So we let Pydantic write them.

167
00:10:16,712 --> 00:10:22,236
Here's a note with a string title, a
string text, a boolean pinned, and a colour

168
00:10:22,236 --> 00:10:24,722
that must be yellow, blue, or green.

169
00:10:25,322 --> 00:10:28,463
Schema for turns the
class into a JSON schema.

170
00:10:28,813 --> 00:10:34,036
Look at the right side: string, string,
boolean, and an enum with the three colours.

171
00:10:34,386 --> 00:10:39,808
Every field is required, and
additional properties is false, which strict mode

172
00:10:39,808 --> 00:10:40,225
needs.

173
00:10:42,025 --> 00:10:43,552
How does it work inside?

174
00:10:43,902 --> 00:10:49,341
Pydantic's schema contains a few
keywords that strict tool schemas don't accept,

175
00:10:49,341 --> 00:10:50,701
like maximum length.

176
00:10:51,051 --> 00:10:52,357
So we remove them.

177
00:10:52,707 --> 00:10:57,599
That's safe, because Pydantic still
checks those limits when the input arrives.

178
00:10:58,199 --> 00:11:01,883
This code walks through the
schema and drops those keywords.

179
00:11:02,233 --> 00:11:08,423
One detail: Pydantic adds a keyword
called title, but our task also has a field

180
00:11:08,423 --> 00:11:09,442
called title.

181
00:11:09,792 --> 00:11:12,125
So property names are kept, always.

182
00:11:12,475 --> 00:11:18,159
I actually got this wrong in my first
version, and a test caught it: the title field

183
00:11:18,159 --> 00:11:18,769
vanished.

184
00:11:19,369 --> 00:11:25,530
Then, for every object, additional
properties becomes false, and every field becomes

185
00:11:25,530 --> 00:11:26,190
required.

186
00:11:26,540 --> 00:11:31,282
Optional values, like the due date,
are written as either a date or null.

187
00:11:31,882 --> 00:11:36,068
And definitions builds the
list for the API, from the registry.

188
00:11:36,418 --> 00:11:40,190
Name, description, schema,
and strict true, for every tool.

189
00:11:41,990 --> 00:11:43,382
Here's the whole menu.

190
00:11:43,732 --> 00:11:48,913
Six tools: add, list, complete and
delete tasks, and add and search notes.

191
00:11:49,263 --> 00:11:52,377
Each with its inputs, its
permission, and its timeout.

192
00:11:52,727 --> 00:11:55,307
One registry, so nothing can drift.

193
00:11:57,107 --> 00:11:58,988
Next, parallel tool calls.

194
00:11:59,338 --> 00:12:01,909
Imagine your table orders three dishes.

195
00:12:02,259 --> 00:12:07,255
A good waiter takes all three
orders in one trip, not one trip per dish.

196
00:12:07,605 --> 00:12:09,257
A model can do the same.

197
00:12:09,607 --> 00:12:15,456
When a request needs several independent
things, one reply can contain several tool

198
00:12:15,456 --> 00:12:16,232
use blocks.

199
00:12:16,582 --> 00:12:20,782
Your code runs them all, and
sends all the results back together.

200
00:12:22,582 --> 00:12:24,627
The request asks for two tasks.

201
00:12:24,977 --> 00:12:27,602
The reply contains two tool use blocks.

202
00:12:27,952 --> 00:12:29,658
That's a parallel tool call.

203
00:12:30,258 --> 00:12:36,366
We run each call in order, and each
result keeps the ID of the call it answers:

204
00:12:36,366 --> 00:12:39,381
offline zero one, and offline zero two.

205
00:12:39,981 --> 00:12:45,944
And here's the rule developers often
break: all the results go into one single user

206
00:12:45,944 --> 00:12:48,530
message, not one message per result.

207
00:12:48,880 --> 00:12:54,141
The next request sends three
messages, and the last one holds both results.

208
00:12:55,941 --> 00:12:57,942
Let's dry run the message list.

209
00:12:58,292 --> 00:13:00,951
Message one, from the user: the request.

210
00:13:01,551 --> 00:13:07,668
Message two, from the assistant: two
tool use blocks, IDs zero one and zero two.

211
00:13:08,268 --> 00:13:14,122
Message three, from the user: both tool
results, zero one and zero two, together.

212
00:13:14,722 --> 00:13:18,773
And the wrong way: two
separate user messages, one per result.

213
00:13:19,123 --> 00:13:20,170
Don't do that.

214
00:13:20,520 --> 00:13:24,178
Keep the results together,
in the same order as the calls.

215
00:13:25,978 --> 00:13:27,470
Pause and work it out.

216
00:13:27,820 --> 00:13:29,940
A request needs three tool calls.

217
00:13:30,290 --> 00:13:34,935
The model asks for all three in
its first reply, and then answers.

218
00:13:35,535 --> 00:13:37,167
How many requests are sent?

219
00:13:37,517 --> 00:13:40,503
And how many messages
are in the list at the end?

220
00:13:47,103 --> 00:13:48,151
Two requests.

221
00:13:48,501 --> 00:13:54,082
And four messages: the user request, the
assistant with three tool calls, the user

222
00:13:54,082 --> 00:13:56,873
with three results, and the final answer.

223
00:13:57,223 --> 00:14:02,836
Compare that with lesson three of
Module 4, where one call per turn needed more

224
00:14:02,836 --> 00:14:03,689
round trips.

225
00:14:05,489 --> 00:14:06,630
Interview corner.

226
00:14:06,980 --> 00:14:10,360
Why generate tool
schemas from Pydantic models?

227
00:14:12,960 --> 00:14:15,330
A good answer: one source of truth.

228
00:14:15,680 --> 00:14:21,416
The same class describes the input to
the model, and validates it in your code, so

229
00:14:21,416 --> 00:14:23,235
the two can't drift apart.

230
00:14:23,585 --> 00:14:26,651
And less hand-written
JSON means fewer mistakes.

231
00:14:27,001 --> 00:14:29,924
The trap: thinking the
schema alone protects you.

232
00:14:30,274 --> 00:14:33,190
You still validate
every input when it arrives.

233
00:14:33,790 --> 00:14:37,361
Follow-up: the model
returns two tool use blocks.

234
00:14:37,711 --> 00:14:39,059
How do you respond?

235
00:14:41,659 --> 00:14:47,830
Run both, then send one user message,
containing two tool results, each with its

236
00:14:47,830 --> 00:14:48,756
matching ID.

237
00:14:49,106 --> 00:14:53,874
The trap: one message per
result, or answering only the first call.

238
00:14:54,224 --> 00:14:55,954
The next request will fail.

239
00:14:57,754 --> 00:14:58,613
Quick check.

240
00:14:58,963 --> 00:15:01,642
A reply contains three tool use blocks.

241
00:15:01,992 --> 00:15:04,252
What does the next user message contain?

242
00:15:10,452 --> 00:15:16,113
Three tool result blocks, each with
its matching ID, all in that one message.

243
00:15:17,913 --> 00:15:18,720
Recap.

244
00:15:19,070 --> 00:15:23,451
A registry lists every tool
once, so there's one source of truth.

245
00:15:24,051 --> 00:15:27,895
The tool decorator
registers a function by its name.

246
00:15:28,495 --> 00:15:31,736
Pydantic classes
generate the strict schemas.

247
00:15:32,336 --> 00:15:38,016
And for parallel calls, run them all,
and return all the results in one message.

248
00:15:38,616 --> 00:15:42,141
Next lesson: the model's
input can still be wrong.

249
00:15:42,491 --> 00:15:47,878
You'll validate every call, and turn
mistakes into messages the model can fix.

250
00:15:49,700 --> 00:15:52,301
Your tool now receives input from a model.

251
00:15:52,651 --> 00:15:58,018
And here's a habit that separates hobby
projects from production code: never trust

252
00:15:58,018 --> 00:15:58,738
that input.

253
00:15:59,088 --> 00:16:00,580
Always check it first.

254
00:16:00,930 --> 00:16:06,610
In this lesson, you'll see what can go
wrong with tool input, what strict mode does

255
00:16:06,610 --> 00:16:12,358
and doesn't protect you from, and how to
turn every mistake into a message the model

256
00:16:12,358 --> 00:16:12,906
can fix.

257
00:16:14,706 --> 00:16:17,429
You'll explain why
tool input is untrusted.

258
00:16:18,029 --> 00:16:21,221
Know what strict mode
guarantees, and what it doesn't.

259
00:16:21,821 --> 00:16:25,601
Validate input with
Pydantic, before running a tool.

260
00:16:26,201 --> 00:16:30,136
And return clear errors, so
the model can correct itself.

261
00:16:31,936 --> 00:16:33,417
Why validate at all?

262
00:16:33,767 --> 00:16:35,252
Think of a bank clerk.

263
00:16:35,602 --> 00:16:37,591
You hand them a form to send money.

264
00:16:37,941 --> 00:16:39,910
Before anything moves, they check it.

265
00:16:40,260 --> 00:16:42,503
Is the account number the right length?

266
00:16:42,853 --> 00:16:44,166
Is the date real?

267
00:16:44,516 --> 00:16:46,207
Is the amount positive?

268
00:16:46,557 --> 00:16:49,757
If something's wrong, they
don't crash, and they don't guess.

269
00:16:50,107 --> 00:16:53,923
They hand the form back, and
tell you exactly what to fix.

270
00:16:54,273 --> 00:16:56,516
Your tools should behave the same way.

271
00:16:57,116 --> 00:17:02,541
Tool input comes from a model, and the
model's input comes, partly, from users.

272
00:17:02,891 --> 00:17:07,793
A model can misread a date,
invent a value, or be tricked by a user.

273
00:17:08,143 --> 00:17:12,461
So treat tool input like any
untrusted input from the internet.

274
00:17:14,262 --> 00:17:18,898
There are two layers of protection,
and it's important to know which does what.

275
00:17:19,248 --> 00:17:24,114
Strict mode makes sure the right
fields are there, with no unknown fields.

276
00:17:24,714 --> 00:17:29,120
It makes sure the types are right,
and that enum values are from the list.

277
00:17:29,720 --> 00:17:34,701
But limits, like a maximum length or
a minimum number, aren't part of strict

278
00:17:34,701 --> 00:17:35,226
schemas.

279
00:17:35,576 --> 00:17:37,640
Pydantic checks those in your code.

280
00:17:38,240 --> 00:17:44,097
And business rules, like the due date not
being in the past, or task seven existing,

281
00:17:44,097 --> 00:17:45,840
only your code can check.

282
00:17:47,640 --> 00:17:49,809
Here's how the project validates.

283
00:17:50,159 --> 00:17:55,697
Pydantic's model validate takes the
raw dictionary from the model, and either

284
00:17:55,697 --> 00:17:59,652
returns a checked object,
or raises a validation error.

285
00:18:00,252 --> 00:18:02,074
If it fails, we don't crash.

286
00:18:02,424 --> 00:18:08,049
We turn every problem into one short
line, the field name and what's wrong, and

287
00:18:08,049 --> 00:18:09,402
raise a tool error.

288
00:18:09,752 --> 00:18:15,578
Remember from Module 4: a tool error
becomes an observation the model can read.

289
00:18:17,378 --> 00:18:20,541
Some rules need context
that a schema can't know.

290
00:18:20,891 --> 00:18:22,717
Is the due date in the past?

291
00:18:23,067 --> 00:18:24,575
That depends on today.

292
00:18:24,925 --> 00:18:30,845
So that check lives inside the tool, and
it raises a tool error with both dates, so

293
00:18:30,845 --> 00:18:33,698
the model knows exactly what went wrong.

294
00:18:35,498 --> 00:18:40,623
Let's send five inputs through the real
run tool function, and see what happens to

295
00:18:40,623 --> 00:18:41,185
each one.

296
00:18:41,785 --> 00:18:45,884
The first input is valid, so
the task is added, as task one.

297
00:18:46,484 --> 00:18:49,170
The second has a due date of next friday.

298
00:18:49,520 --> 00:18:54,685
That's not a date in the right format,
so we get a clear message about the due

299
00:18:54,685 --> 00:18:55,082
field.

300
00:18:55,682 --> 00:19:01,057
The third says urgent, which isn't one
of the allowed priorities, and the message

301
00:19:01,057 --> 00:19:02,715
lists the three that are.

302
00:19:03,315 --> 00:19:07,314
The fourth has an empty title,
which breaks the minimum length.

303
00:19:07,914 --> 00:19:13,566
And the fifth has a perfectly valid
shape, but the date is in January, already in

304
00:19:13,566 --> 00:19:14,194
the past.

305
00:19:14,544 --> 00:19:16,575
Only the business rule catches it.

306
00:19:18,375 --> 00:19:20,954
Now the most useful part: recovery.

307
00:19:21,304 --> 00:19:23,917
Turn one: the model sends a bad call.

308
00:19:24,267 --> 00:19:29,702
Our code returns an error result,
marked is error, that names both problems.

309
00:19:30,302 --> 00:19:35,981
Turn two: the model reads the message,
fixes both fields, and the call succeeds.

310
00:19:36,581 --> 00:19:39,223
Turn three: the model answers the user.

311
00:19:41,023 --> 00:19:44,139
Let's watch that
recovery in the real agent.

312
00:19:44,489 --> 00:19:46,155
First, an empty store.

313
00:19:46,755 --> 00:19:50,852
Here the offline fake is
scripted to send a bad call first.

314
00:19:51,202 --> 00:19:53,870
Turn one: the error, with both problems.

315
00:19:54,220 --> 00:19:57,412
Turn two: the corrected
call, and the task is added.

316
00:19:57,762 --> 00:20:03,603
With a real model, this recovery isn't
scripted: the model reads your message and

317
00:20:03,603 --> 00:20:05,262
fixes the input itself.

318
00:20:05,612 --> 00:20:08,186
That's why clear messages matter so much.

319
00:20:09,986 --> 00:20:11,303
Pause and decide.

320
00:20:11,653 --> 00:20:16,179
For each problem, which layer
catches it: strict mode, or your code?

321
00:20:16,779 --> 00:20:17,418
One.

322
00:20:17,768 --> 00:20:21,987
The model sends a field called
deadline, that isn't in the schema.

323
00:20:22,587 --> 00:20:23,209
Two.

324
00:20:23,559 --> 00:20:28,500
The model asks to complete task
forty-two, but there are only three tasks.

325
00:20:29,100 --> 00:20:29,797
Three.

326
00:20:30,147 --> 00:20:34,849
A note's text is five thousand
characters, and the limit is two thousand.

327
00:20:41,449 --> 00:20:45,194
One: strict mode, because
additional properties is false.

328
00:20:45,544 --> 00:20:50,064
Two: your code, because only
the store knows which tasks exist.

329
00:20:50,414 --> 00:20:55,532
Three: your code too, because length
limits aren't part of strict schemas, so

330
00:20:55,532 --> 00:20:56,928
Pydantic checks them.

331
00:20:58,728 --> 00:20:59,869
Interview corner.

332
00:21:00,219 --> 00:21:04,964
If you use strict tool schemas, do
you still need to validate tool inputs?

333
00:21:07,564 --> 00:21:08,249
Yes.

334
00:21:08,599 --> 00:21:11,361
Strict mode guarantees
the shape and the types.

335
00:21:11,711 --> 00:21:17,447
It can't check limits that strict schemas
leave out, or business rules, like the due

336
00:21:17,447 --> 00:21:21,067
date being in the future,
or task forty-two existing.

337
00:21:21,417 --> 00:21:23,699
The trap is saying strict means correct.

338
00:21:24,049 --> 00:21:25,669
Strict means well-formed.

339
00:21:26,269 --> 00:21:30,473
Follow-up: what should a tool
return when its input is invalid?

340
00:21:33,073 --> 00:21:38,603
A tool result marked is error, with a
short, specific message: which field is wrong,

341
00:21:38,603 --> 00:21:39,854
and what's allowed.

342
00:21:40,204 --> 00:21:42,233
Then the model can fix the call.

343
00:21:42,583 --> 00:21:48,096
The trap: crashing the loop with an
exception, or a vague message like invalid

344
00:21:48,096 --> 00:21:50,640
input, which the model can't act on.

345
00:21:52,440 --> 00:21:53,299
Quick check.

346
00:21:53,649 --> 00:21:57,236
Which of these can only
your code catch, not strict mode?

347
00:22:03,436 --> 00:22:05,632
A due date that's already in the past.

348
00:22:05,982 --> 00:22:08,041
It's a perfectly well-formed date.

349
00:22:08,391 --> 00:22:11,068
Only your code knows today, and the rule.

350
00:22:12,868 --> 00:22:13,676
Recap.

351
00:22:14,026 --> 00:22:18,412
Tool input is untrusted, so
check it before running anything.

352
00:22:19,012 --> 00:22:21,176
Strict mode covers shape and types.

353
00:22:21,526 --> 00:22:24,323
Your code covers
limits and business rules.

354
00:22:24,923 --> 00:22:29,769
Pydantic's model validate turns
raw input into a checked object.

355
00:22:30,369 --> 00:22:35,241
And error messages name the field and
what's allowed, so the model can fix it.

356
00:22:35,841 --> 00:22:39,336
Next lesson: tools that
are slow, or never answer.

357
00:22:39,686 --> 00:22:43,731
You'll add timeouts, so one
stuck tool can't freeze your agent.

358
00:22:45,551 --> 00:22:51,438
Real tools talk to the outside world: a
database, a calendar service, a website.

359
00:22:51,788 --> 00:22:56,806
And the outside world is sometimes
slow, and sometimes doesn't answer at all.

360
00:22:57,156 --> 00:23:02,361
In this lesson you'll make sure one
stuck tool can never freeze your whole agent.

361
00:23:04,161 --> 00:23:06,974
You'll explain why
every tool needs a timeout.

362
00:23:07,574 --> 00:23:10,607
Run a function with a
timeout, using a thread.

363
00:23:11,207 --> 00:23:14,600
Turn timeouts and other
failures into tool errors.

364
00:23:15,200 --> 00:23:17,426
And know the limits of thread timeouts.

365
00:23:19,226 --> 00:23:20,850
Why do we need timeouts?

366
00:23:21,200 --> 00:23:23,415
Think of standing in a queue at an office.

367
00:23:23,765 --> 00:23:29,049
If nothing moves for an hour, at some
point you decide to leave, and come back

368
00:23:29,049 --> 00:23:29,862
another day.

369
00:23:30,212 --> 00:23:31,737
You don't wait there forever.

370
00:23:32,087 --> 00:23:35,257
A timeout is that
decision, made in advance.

371
00:23:35,607 --> 00:23:37,544
Wait at most, say, two seconds.

372
00:23:37,894 --> 00:23:40,044
Then move on, with a clear message.

373
00:23:40,644 --> 00:23:46,787
Without a timeout, one tool that never
answers means the agent never answers, the

374
00:23:46,787 --> 00:23:52,929
user waits forever, and in a server,
that request holds resources until something

375
00:23:52,929 --> 00:23:53,536
crashes.

376
00:23:55,336 --> 00:23:57,683
Here's the idea in its simplest form.

377
00:23:58,033 --> 00:24:00,139
We run the tool in a separate thread.

378
00:24:00,489 --> 00:24:05,974
A thread is a second worker inside the
same program, so our main code can keep an

379
00:24:05,974 --> 00:24:07,125
eye on the clock.

380
00:24:07,725 --> 00:24:12,949
Join with a number of seconds means:
wait for the worker to finish, but no longer

381
00:24:12,949 --> 00:24:13,594
than this.

382
00:24:14,194 --> 00:24:19,322
If the worker is still running after
that, we give up, and return a clear error

383
00:24:19,322 --> 00:24:21,594
message instead of waiting forever.

384
00:24:22,194 --> 00:24:26,219
A quick tool finishes in
time, so we get its normal result.

385
00:24:26,819 --> 00:24:31,472
This tool sleeps for sixty
seconds, like a server that never answers.

386
00:24:31,822 --> 00:24:35,512
After two seconds, we stop
waiting, and get a timeout error.

387
00:24:36,112 --> 00:24:39,517
And the total wait was
two seconds, not sixty-two.

388
00:24:39,867 --> 00:24:41,707
The agent stays responsive.

389
00:24:43,507 --> 00:24:46,530
The project's version
adds one important thing.

390
00:24:46,880 --> 00:24:51,442
The worker stores either the
result, or any exception the tool raised.

391
00:24:52,042 --> 00:24:53,538
The thread is a daemon.

392
00:24:53,888 --> 00:24:58,014
That means a stuck worker
never stops the program from exiting.

393
00:24:58,364 --> 00:25:03,544
After the wait, if it's still alive, we
raise a tool error naming the tool and the

394
00:25:03,544 --> 00:25:03,923
limit.

395
00:25:04,523 --> 00:25:09,893
If the tool raised an error, we raise
it again here, in the main thread, so it's

396
00:25:09,893 --> 00:25:11,840
handled like any other error.

397
00:25:12,190 --> 00:25:14,203
Otherwise we return the result.

398
00:25:16,003 --> 00:25:19,134
And here's the function
every tool call goes through.

399
00:25:19,484 --> 00:25:21,312
First: does the tool exist?

400
00:25:21,912 --> 00:25:24,767
Then: is the input
valid, from lesson three.

401
00:25:25,117 --> 00:25:27,940
And is the call
allowed, which is next lesson.

402
00:25:28,540 --> 00:25:31,177
Then: run the tool, with its own timeout.

403
00:25:31,527 --> 00:25:36,700
And a lookup error, like a task that
doesn't exist, becomes a tool error too.

404
00:25:37,050 --> 00:25:40,603
Every problem ends up as
a message, never a crash.

405
00:25:42,403 --> 00:25:45,224
Let's see what the
model receives in each case.

406
00:25:45,574 --> 00:25:48,073
The tool finished: the normal result.

407
00:25:48,673 --> 00:25:54,539
The tool was too slow: search notes timed
out after two seconds, marked as an error.

408
00:25:55,139 --> 00:25:58,424
The task doesn't
exist: there is no task nine.

409
00:25:59,024 --> 00:26:02,897
And bad input: the
validation message from lesson three.

410
00:26:04,697 --> 00:26:06,238
Here's the real agent.

411
00:26:06,588 --> 00:26:11,661
This environment variable makes search
notes sleep for thirty seconds, to play a

412
00:26:11,661 --> 00:26:12,485
slow service.

413
00:26:12,835 --> 00:26:14,651
Its timeout is two seconds.

414
00:26:15,001 --> 00:26:20,365
After two seconds the agent gets a
timeout observation, and finishes normally,

415
00:26:20,365 --> 00:26:21,672
instead of hanging.

416
00:26:23,472 --> 00:26:25,496
Let's be honest about the limits.

417
00:26:25,846 --> 00:26:31,820
A thread timeout can stop waiting after
N seconds, keep the agent responsive, and

418
00:26:31,820 --> 00:26:33,369
report a clear error.

419
00:26:33,969 --> 00:26:35,756
But it can't kill the stuck thread.

420
00:26:36,106 --> 00:26:41,174
In Python, a thread can't be stopped
from outside, so it keeps running in the

421
00:26:41,174 --> 00:26:41,898
background.

422
00:26:42,248 --> 00:26:44,409
It can't undo half-finished work.

423
00:26:44,759 --> 00:26:49,189
And it can't protect you from a
tool that uses huge amounts of memory.

424
00:26:49,539 --> 00:26:55,261
For real isolation, run risky tools
in a separate process, or a container.

425
00:26:55,611 --> 00:27:01,841
And for network calls, use the
library's own timeout too, like the SDK's timeout

426
00:27:01,841 --> 00:27:03,554
setting from Module 2.

427
00:27:05,354 --> 00:27:08,512
Pause and choose a
timeout for each of these tools.

428
00:27:09,112 --> 00:27:09,750
One.

429
00:27:10,100 --> 00:27:13,038
List tasks, which
reads a small local file.

430
00:27:13,638 --> 00:27:14,260
Two.

431
00:27:14,610 --> 00:27:17,699
Search notes, which
calls a remote search service.

432
00:27:18,299 --> 00:27:18,996
Three.

433
00:27:19,346 --> 00:27:24,897
Export report, which builds a PDF
that usually takes about twenty seconds.

434
00:27:31,497 --> 00:27:34,889
There's no single right
number, but here's the reasoning.

435
00:27:35,239 --> 00:27:39,721
A local file should take
milliseconds, so a few seconds is generous.

436
00:27:40,071 --> 00:27:45,017
A remote service: a few seconds, so a
slow network doesn't freeze the agent.

437
00:27:45,367 --> 00:27:50,679
A twenty-second job needs more,
perhaps sixty seconds, or better, run it in the

438
00:27:50,679 --> 00:27:53,840
background and let the
agent check on it later.

439
00:27:55,640 --> 00:27:56,781
Interview corner.

440
00:27:57,131 --> 00:28:00,131
How do you stop a slow
tool from freezing an agent?

441
00:28:02,731 --> 00:28:04,499
Give every tool a timeout.

442
00:28:04,849 --> 00:28:10,796
Run it in a worker, a thread or a
process, wait at most N seconds, and then return

443
00:28:10,796 --> 00:28:11,956
an error result.

444
00:28:12,306 --> 00:28:16,195
For network calls, also
use the library's own timeout.

445
00:28:16,545 --> 00:28:19,837
The trap: expecting the
model to notice it's slow.

446
00:28:20,187 --> 00:28:23,420
The model can only act
after your code returns.

447
00:28:24,020 --> 00:28:27,080
Follow-up: would you
use a thread or a process?

448
00:28:29,680 --> 00:28:34,862
A thread is simple, and fine for
waiting on input and output, but it can't be

449
00:28:34,862 --> 00:28:35,333
killed.

450
00:28:35,683 --> 00:28:41,636
A process can be terminated, and it
isolates memory and crashes, but it costs more

451
00:28:41,636 --> 00:28:42,289
to start.

452
00:28:42,639 --> 00:28:45,939
The trap: claiming a
thread timeout stops the work.

453
00:28:46,289 --> 00:28:47,915
It only stops the waiting.

454
00:28:49,715 --> 00:28:50,573
Quick check.

455
00:28:50,923 --> 00:28:54,930
After a thread timeout fires,
what happens to the stuck tool?

456
00:29:01,130 --> 00:29:03,117
It keeps running in the background.

457
00:29:03,467 --> 00:29:05,044
We just stop waiting for it.

458
00:29:05,394 --> 00:29:09,293
If you must be able to stop
the work, use a separate process.

459
00:29:11,093 --> 00:29:11,901
Recap.

460
00:29:12,251 --> 00:29:16,314
Every tool gets a timeout, so
the agent never waits forever.

461
00:29:16,914 --> 00:29:20,546
A worker thread, plus join
with seconds, stops the waiting.

462
00:29:21,146 --> 00:29:25,579
Timeouts, missing items and
bad input all become tool errors.

463
00:29:26,179 --> 00:29:30,742
And threads can't be killed, so
use processes for real isolation.

464
00:29:31,342 --> 00:29:35,062
Next lesson: the most
important safety feature of all.

465
00:29:35,412 --> 00:29:39,973
Permissions, and asking a human
before anything dangerous happens.

466
00:29:41,786 --> 00:29:45,063
So far, every valid tool call simply runs.

467
00:29:45,413 --> 00:29:47,350
That's fine for listing tasks.

468
00:29:47,700 --> 00:29:52,618
It's not fine for deleting your
data, sending an email, or paying a bill.

469
00:29:52,968 --> 00:29:58,861
In this lesson you'll give every tool a
permission level, add a read-only mode, and

470
00:29:58,861 --> 00:30:03,192
make the agent ask a human
before anything dangerous happens.

471
00:30:04,992 --> 00:30:08,451
You'll explain the principle
of least privilege for agents.

472
00:30:09,051 --> 00:30:11,999
Sort tools into read, write, and danger.

473
00:30:12,599 --> 00:30:14,726
Block writes in a read-only mode.

474
00:30:15,326 --> 00:30:18,432
And ask a human before
every dangerous action.

475
00:30:20,232 --> 00:30:25,463
Let's start with an idea security
engineers use everywhere: least privilege.

476
00:30:25,813 --> 00:30:27,596
Think of a hotel key card.

477
00:30:27,946 --> 00:30:30,324
It doesn't open
every door in the building.

478
00:30:30,674 --> 00:30:32,831
It opens your room, for your stay.

479
00:30:33,181 --> 00:30:35,264
If you lose it, the damage is small.

480
00:30:35,614 --> 00:30:41,681
For agents, least privilege means: give
the agent only the tools and powers it needs

481
00:30:41,681 --> 00:30:43,920
for this job, and nothing more.

482
00:30:44,520 --> 00:30:46,950
Why does this matter so much for agents?

483
00:30:47,300 --> 00:30:53,260
Because a model can misunderstand a
request, and a user, or text inside a document,

484
00:30:53,260 --> 00:30:56,778
can try to trick it
into doing something harmful.

485
00:30:57,128 --> 00:31:01,112
Permissions limit the damage,
even when the model gets it wrong.

486
00:31:02,912 --> 00:31:05,037
Our project uses three levels.

487
00:31:05,387 --> 00:31:07,719
Read: list tasks and search notes.

488
00:31:08,069 --> 00:31:12,206
These are always allowed,
because looking never changes anything.

489
00:31:12,806 --> 00:31:15,827
Write: add task,
complete task, and add note.

490
00:31:16,177 --> 00:31:21,990
These change data, so they're allowed,
unless the agent runs in read-only mode.

491
00:31:22,590 --> 00:31:24,431
And danger: delete task.

492
00:31:24,781 --> 00:31:29,645
Deleting can't be undone, so a
human must approve every single call.

493
00:31:31,445 --> 00:31:32,812
Here's the policy.

494
00:31:33,162 --> 00:31:39,760
It holds the context, a read-only flag,
and ask human: a function that asks a person

495
00:31:39,760 --> 00:31:41,409
a yes or no question.

496
00:31:41,759 --> 00:31:46,436
If there's nobody to ask, it's
None, and dangerous calls are denied.

497
00:31:46,786 --> 00:31:49,390
It also keeps a log of every decision.

498
00:31:49,990 --> 00:31:52,215
The check runs before every call.

499
00:31:52,565 --> 00:31:55,467
Read tools return straight away: allowed.

500
00:31:56,067 --> 00:32:02,141
In read-only mode, any other tool is
blocked, with a clear reason the model can pass

501
00:32:02,141 --> 00:32:03,225
on to the user.

502
00:32:03,825 --> 00:32:09,340
For danger tools, we build a clear
question, using the tool's confirm function.

503
00:32:09,690 --> 00:32:15,480
For delete task, it says which task, by
number and title, so the human knows exactly

504
00:32:15,480 --> 00:32:17,065
what they're approving.

505
00:32:17,665 --> 00:32:18,741
Then we ask.

506
00:32:19,091 --> 00:32:21,237
Anything other than yes means no.

507
00:32:21,587 --> 00:32:27,352
The decision is logged, and if it's
declined, the model gets a tool error that says:

508
00:32:27,352 --> 00:32:29,685
the user declined, don't retry it.

509
00:32:31,485 --> 00:32:33,086
Let's try four calls.

510
00:32:33,436 --> 00:32:36,646
In read-only mode,
listing tasks is allowed.

511
00:32:37,246 --> 00:32:40,835
But completing a task is
blocked, and nothing changes.

512
00:32:41,436 --> 00:32:43,014
Now a dangerous call.

513
00:32:43,364 --> 00:32:47,250
The policy asks the
human: delete task one, pay rent?

514
00:32:47,600 --> 00:32:51,939
This human says no, so the call
is declined, and the task stays.

515
00:32:52,539 --> 00:32:57,027
And when the human says yes, only
then does the delete actually run.

516
00:32:58,827 --> 00:33:00,806
The order of these checks matters.

517
00:33:01,156 --> 00:33:02,907
First, does the tool exist?

518
00:33:03,257 --> 00:33:04,362
If not, error.

519
00:33:04,962 --> 00:33:06,748
Second, is the input valid?

520
00:33:07,098 --> 00:33:09,790
We never ask a human to approve nonsense.

521
00:33:10,390 --> 00:33:13,139
Third, for a delete, does the task exist?

522
00:33:13,489 --> 00:33:15,528
We need it to write a clear question.

523
00:33:16,128 --> 00:33:21,021
Fourth, the permission check, which
asks the human only for danger tools.

524
00:33:21,621 --> 00:33:24,025
And fifth, run the tool with its timeout.

525
00:33:25,825 --> 00:33:27,473
Now in the real agent.

526
00:33:27,823 --> 00:33:31,304
An empty store, and two
tasks added, in one turn.

527
00:33:31,904 --> 00:33:33,279
Delete task one.

528
00:33:33,629 --> 00:33:35,413
The agent stops and asks.

529
00:33:35,763 --> 00:33:39,836
Here I pipe in the answer no,
so you can see it in the recording.

530
00:33:40,186 --> 00:33:43,216
The delete is
declined, and the task is safe.

531
00:33:43,816 --> 00:33:47,664
And in read-only mode,
even adding a task is blocked.

532
00:33:48,014 --> 00:33:52,844
That's a good default for an agent
that should only answer questions about your

533
00:33:52,844 --> 00:33:53,150
data.

534
00:33:54,950 --> 00:33:57,268
One warning about confirmations.

535
00:33:57,618 --> 00:34:04,010
A question like, allow delete task, task
ID one, teaches people to click yes without

536
00:34:04,010 --> 00:34:04,618
reading.

537
00:34:04,968 --> 00:34:11,000
A good question shows exactly what will
happen, in plain words: delete task one, pay

538
00:34:11,000 --> 00:34:12,436
rent, high priority.

539
00:34:12,786 --> 00:34:18,469
Ask for every dangerous call, not just
the first, and treat no answer as a no.

540
00:34:20,269 --> 00:34:23,797
Pause and choose a permission
level for each of these tools.

541
00:34:24,397 --> 00:34:25,035
One.

542
00:34:25,385 --> 00:34:26,812
Get weather, for a city.

543
00:34:27,412 --> 00:34:28,033
Two.

544
00:34:28,383 --> 00:34:29,524
Rename task.

545
00:34:30,124 --> 00:34:30,821
Three.

546
00:34:31,171 --> 00:34:32,233
Send email.

547
00:34:38,833 --> 00:34:41,239
Get weather is read: it only looks.

548
00:34:41,589 --> 00:34:46,212
Rename task is write: it
changes data, but it's easy to undo.

549
00:34:46,562 --> 00:34:51,782
Send email is danger: once it's sent,
you can't take it back, so a human should

550
00:34:51,782 --> 00:34:52,972
approve every one.

551
00:34:54,772 --> 00:34:55,913
Interview corner.

552
00:34:56,263 --> 00:34:58,808
How do you make a tool-using agent safe?

553
00:35:01,408 --> 00:35:04,574
Least privilege, so
only the tools it needs.

554
00:35:04,924 --> 00:35:06,131
Permission levels.

555
00:35:06,481 --> 00:35:08,144
Validate every input.

556
00:35:08,494 --> 00:35:09,363
Timeouts.

557
00:35:09,713 --> 00:35:15,557
And human approval for actions that can't
be undone, with a clear confirmation and a

558
00:35:15,557 --> 00:35:17,088
log of every decision.

559
00:35:17,438 --> 00:35:21,182
The trap: saying, tell the
model in the prompt to be careful.

560
00:35:21,532 --> 00:35:23,037
Prompts guide the model.

561
00:35:23,387 --> 00:35:25,604
Only code enforces the rules.

562
00:35:26,204 --> 00:35:29,127
Follow-up: where should
the approval check live?

563
00:35:31,727 --> 00:35:37,668
In your code, between validation and
execution, so it runs no matter what the model

564
00:35:37,668 --> 00:35:38,026
says.

565
00:35:38,376 --> 00:35:43,276
You can tell the model about it, but
never rely on the model to enforce it.

566
00:35:43,626 --> 00:35:48,337
The trap: asking the model to ask
the user for permission in its reply.

567
00:35:50,137 --> 00:35:50,996
Quick check.

568
00:35:51,346 --> 00:35:55,782
Why does the policy validate
the input before asking the human?

569
00:36:01,982 --> 00:36:06,064
So a human only ever
approves a real, well-formed action.

570
00:36:06,414 --> 00:36:11,830
Asking about nonsense wastes their
attention, and teaches them to click yes without

571
00:36:11,830 --> 00:36:12,352
reading.

572
00:36:14,152 --> 00:36:14,960
Recap.

573
00:36:15,310 --> 00:36:19,519
Least privilege: only the
tools and powers the job needs.

574
00:36:20,119 --> 00:36:25,736
Read is always allowed, write unless
read-only, and danger needs a human yes.

575
00:36:26,336 --> 00:36:29,605
Validate first, then
ask, with a clear question.

576
00:36:30,205 --> 00:36:33,589
And enforce it in code,
never only in the prompt.

577
00:36:34,189 --> 00:36:36,702
You now know every idea in this module.

578
00:36:37,052 --> 00:36:42,381
Next lesson, we put them together,
and build the personal productivity agent.

579
00:36:44,186 --> 00:36:45,344
Project time.

580
00:36:45,694 --> 00:36:51,414
Over two lessons we'll build a personal
productivity agent: you tell it, in plain

581
00:36:51,414 --> 00:36:57,346
words, what to do with your tasks and
notes, and it uses six tools to do it, safely.

582
00:36:57,696 --> 00:37:00,585
Everything from this
module comes together here.

583
00:37:00,935 --> 00:37:02,729
We'll go one file at a time.

584
00:37:04,529 --> 00:37:05,942
Here's what we're building.

585
00:37:06,292 --> 00:37:11,587
You ask in plain words, at the command
line, like: add task pay rent, and list

586
00:37:11,587 --> 00:37:11,995
tasks.

587
00:37:12,595 --> 00:37:18,469
The agent has six tools, for tasks and
notes, defined once in a registry, with

588
00:37:18,469 --> 00:37:20,125
schemas from Pydantic.

589
00:37:20,725 --> 00:37:24,772
Every input is validated,
and every tool has a timeout.

590
00:37:25,372 --> 00:37:31,313
Tools have permission levels, there's a
read-only mode, and deleting needs a human

591
00:37:31,313 --> 00:37:31,603
yes.

592
00:37:32,203 --> 00:37:37,983
And the data is saved safely in a JSON
file, with forty-three tests and an offline

593
00:37:37,983 --> 00:37:38,336
mode.

594
00:37:40,136 --> 00:37:41,265
Here's the plan.

595
00:37:41,615 --> 00:37:47,227
The command line passes your request to
the agent, which runs the loop from Module

596
00:37:47,227 --> 00:37:47,364
4.

597
00:37:47,964 --> 00:37:54,120
Every tool call goes through tools dot
py: the registry, validation, and timeouts.

598
00:37:54,470 --> 00:37:59,269
It asks the policy for permission,
and the tools read and write the store.

599
00:37:59,869 --> 00:38:05,898
And the agent talks to the model through
the client: the real Claude API with a key,

600
00:38:05,898 --> 00:38:08,195
or the offline fake without one.

601
00:38:09,995 --> 00:38:14,726
First, so you know exactly what you're
seeing in this video: the offline fake.

602
00:38:15,076 --> 00:38:21,876
It speaks the same protocol as the real
API, so the SDK and the loop run unchanged.

603
00:38:22,226 --> 00:38:27,844
It splits your request on the word and,
matches each part against a few patterns,

604
00:38:27,844 --> 00:38:31,174
and asks for all the
matching tools in one turn.

605
00:38:31,774 --> 00:38:33,028
That's not a model.

606
00:38:33,378 --> 00:38:38,524
It can't understand free-form
requests, so the demos use simple phrases.

607
00:38:38,874 --> 00:38:41,711
No API key was used in this recording.

608
00:38:42,061 --> 00:38:47,642
The tools, the validation, the
timeouts, and the policy are all completely real.

609
00:38:49,442 --> 00:38:52,176
Now the store, where tasks and notes live.

610
00:38:52,526 --> 00:38:54,499
Imagine editing a notebook.

611
00:38:54,849 --> 00:39:00,241
If you tear out a page, and then get
interrupted before writing the new one, the

612
00:39:00,241 --> 00:39:01,454
page is just gone.

613
00:39:01,804 --> 00:39:05,975
The safe way is to write the
new page first, and then swap it in.

614
00:39:06,325 --> 00:39:08,141
That's called an atomic save.

615
00:39:08,491 --> 00:39:13,826
At any moment, you have either the old
file or the new file, never half of one.

616
00:39:15,626 --> 00:39:17,656
Load reads the JSON file.

617
00:39:18,006 --> 00:39:22,539
If there's no file yet, we start
with empty lists of tasks and notes.

618
00:39:23,139 --> 00:39:29,226
Save writes everything to a temporary
file, then os dot replace swaps it in, in one

619
00:39:29,226 --> 00:39:29,592
step.

620
00:39:29,942 --> 00:39:34,843
If the program crashes halfway
through writing, the real file is untouched.

621
00:39:35,443 --> 00:39:40,346
New items get the next ID: one
more than the biggest ID so far.

622
00:39:40,946 --> 00:39:45,943
And every change follows the
same three steps: load, modify, save.

623
00:39:46,293 --> 00:39:50,850
Add task creates a task with
the next ID, appends it, and saves.

624
00:39:52,650 --> 00:39:54,067
Let's look at the tools.

625
00:39:54,417 --> 00:39:56,200
List tasks is a read tool.

626
00:39:56,550 --> 00:40:02,072
Its description tells the model to use it
before completing or deleting, to find the

627
00:40:02,072 --> 00:40:02,993
task's number.

628
00:40:03,593 --> 00:40:05,595
Complete task is a write tool.

629
00:40:05,945 --> 00:40:08,066
It needs the number from list tasks.

630
00:40:08,666 --> 00:40:10,346
Delete task is danger.

631
00:40:10,696 --> 00:40:16,244
Its description warns the model to use
it only when the user clearly asks, and to

632
00:40:16,244 --> 00:40:18,641
prefer complete task for finishing.

633
00:40:18,991 --> 00:40:23,695
And it has a confirm function,
which builds the question the human sees.

634
00:40:24,295 --> 00:40:28,653
Add note is a write tool, that
saves an idea or meeting notes.

635
00:40:29,253 --> 00:40:34,813
And search notes is read, with a
shorter, two-second timeout, because in a real app

636
00:40:34,813 --> 00:40:37,358
it would call a remote search service.

637
00:40:39,158 --> 00:40:41,910
Here's how the command
line wires it together.

638
00:40:42,260 --> 00:40:48,098
Ask human prints the question, with y
slash capital N, so no is the default, and

639
00:40:48,098 --> 00:40:49,192
reads one line.

640
00:40:49,542 --> 00:40:53,216
Piped input works too,
which is how the demos answer.

641
00:40:53,816 --> 00:40:57,277
For each request, we build
the context, and the policy.

642
00:40:57,627 --> 00:41:00,341
The read-only flag sets read-only mode.

643
00:41:00,691 --> 00:41:05,992
And the yes flag approves dangerous
actions automatically, which is useful for

644
00:41:05,992 --> 00:41:09,187
scripts you trust, and
dangerous anywhere else.

645
00:41:09,787 --> 00:41:15,501
Then the agent gets the client, the cost
tracker, the context, and the policy, and

646
00:41:15,501 --> 00:41:19,055
runs the request,
printing each step as it happens.

647
00:41:19,655 --> 00:41:24,155
Finally, a footer with turns,
tool calls and cost, and the answer.

648
00:41:24,505 --> 00:41:30,148
Any ending other than answered becomes
one clear error, exactly like Module 4.

649
00:41:31,948 --> 00:41:32,886
Let's run it.

650
00:41:33,236 --> 00:41:37,537
The tools command shows every
tool, with its permission and timeout.

651
00:41:38,137 --> 00:41:40,301
Reset gives us an empty store.

652
00:41:40,651 --> 00:41:44,634
It deletes data, so it asks
first too, unless we pass yes.

653
00:41:45,234 --> 00:41:47,506
And a first request with three parts.

654
00:41:47,856 --> 00:41:51,239
Look at the trace: three
tool calls, all in turn one.

655
00:41:51,589 --> 00:41:53,295
That's a parallel tool call.

656
00:41:53,645 --> 00:41:57,324
Two tasks and a note,
saved, in two turns in total.

657
00:41:59,124 --> 00:42:00,433
Pause and predict.

658
00:42:00,783 --> 00:42:02,726
The store has tasks one and two.

659
00:42:03,076 --> 00:42:07,236
The request is: complete
task two, and delete task seven.

660
00:42:07,586 --> 00:42:09,633
The human would say yes if asked.

661
00:42:10,233 --> 00:42:11,941
How many tool calls are there?

662
00:42:12,291 --> 00:42:13,655
Is the human asked?

663
00:42:14,005 --> 00:42:15,860
And what does each call return?

664
00:42:22,460 --> 00:42:24,688
Two tool calls, both in turn one.

665
00:42:25,038 --> 00:42:26,806
Complete task two succeeds.

666
00:42:27,156 --> 00:42:32,596
Delete task seven fails before anyone
is asked, with: there is no task seven.

667
00:42:32,946 --> 00:42:36,680
The human is never asked
about a task that doesn't exist.

668
00:42:38,480 --> 00:42:39,287
Recap.

669
00:42:39,637 --> 00:42:43,500
An honest fake: a script
that speaks the real protocol.

670
00:42:44,100 --> 00:42:47,919
An atomic store: write a
temporary file, then replace.

671
00:42:48,519 --> 00:42:52,454
Six tools, with clear
descriptions and three permission levels.

672
00:42:53,054 --> 00:42:57,240
And the command line builds
the policy from read-only and yes.

673
00:42:57,840 --> 00:43:04,056
In part two: the agent loop with parallel
calls, the full demo, failures on purpose,

674
00:43:04,056 --> 00:43:06,349
the tests, and the module quiz.

675
00:43:08,165 --> 00:43:09,225
Welcome back.

676
00:43:09,575 --> 00:43:12,460
The store, the tools
and the policy are ready.

677
00:43:12,810 --> 00:43:18,455
Now we connect them in the agent loop,
run a full demo, break things on purpose, and

678
00:43:18,455 --> 00:43:19,665
look at the tests.

679
00:43:20,015 --> 00:43:25,586
Then we'll wrap up the module with
interview questions, a quiz, and your extension

680
00:43:25,586 --> 00:43:26,266
challenge.

681
00:43:28,066 --> 00:43:29,693
First, the system prompt.

682
00:43:30,043 --> 00:43:34,815
Notice the style from Module
3: it explains why, not just what.

683
00:43:35,165 --> 00:43:40,992
The tools are the only way to read or
change the data, so the model has a reason to

684
00:43:40,992 --> 00:43:41,624
use them.

685
00:43:42,224 --> 00:43:47,541
It asks for parallel tool calls when
the user wants several independent things.

686
00:43:48,141 --> 00:43:53,901
And it says how to behave: look up a
task's number first, fix the input after an

687
00:43:53,901 --> 00:43:57,573
error, and never retry
an action the user declined.

688
00:43:57,923 --> 00:44:02,388
Today's date is filled in, so the
model can write due dates correctly.

689
00:44:04,188 --> 00:44:08,119
Act runs one tool call
through run tool, with the policy.

690
00:44:08,469 --> 00:44:14,717
Any tool error, invalid input, a denied
action, a timeout, becomes a step marked as

691
00:44:14,717 --> 00:44:15,394
an error.

692
00:44:15,744 --> 00:44:16,923
Never a crash.

693
00:44:17,523 --> 00:44:23,557
Run is the loop you know from Module
4: a step limit, a budget check, and clean

694
00:44:23,557 --> 00:44:26,536
stops for a refusal or a cut-off reply.

695
00:44:27,136 --> 00:44:30,209
No tool calls means
the model has answered.

696
00:44:30,809 --> 00:44:32,107
And here's what's new.

697
00:44:32,457 --> 00:44:38,436
We act on every tool call in the reply,
in order, report each step to the trace, and

698
00:44:38,436 --> 00:44:39,859
collect the results.

699
00:44:40,209 --> 00:44:45,880
Then all the results go back in one
single user message, exactly as lesson two

700
00:44:45,880 --> 00:44:46,389
showed.

701
00:44:48,189 --> 00:44:50,680
Now a full demo, from an empty store.

702
00:44:51,280 --> 00:44:52,409
First, reset.

703
00:44:53,009 --> 00:44:54,956
Three tasks in one request.

704
00:44:55,306 --> 00:44:59,764
One turn with three parallel
calls, and each task gets its number.

705
00:45:00,364 --> 00:45:04,113
Complete task three, and
save a note, again in one turn.

706
00:45:04,713 --> 00:45:10,608
And two read tools together: all tasks,
with buy milk marked done, and the note we

707
00:45:10,608 --> 00:45:11,399
just saved.

708
00:45:13,199 --> 00:45:15,602
Now the safety features, on purpose.

709
00:45:15,952 --> 00:45:20,379
Delete task one: the agent asks,
with the task's title and due date.

710
00:45:20,729 --> 00:45:23,310
The answer is no, so pay rent stays.

711
00:45:23,910 --> 00:45:28,452
Delete task two, and this time
the answer is yes, so it's deleted.

712
00:45:29,052 --> 00:45:31,674
In read-only mode, writes are blocked.

713
00:45:32,274 --> 00:45:37,482
And a rejected API key ends with
one clear line, and exit code one.

714
00:45:37,832 --> 00:45:39,428
No traceback anywhere.

715
00:45:41,228 --> 00:45:47,104
The tests run the real loop, the real
tools and the real policy, against the fake

716
00:45:47,104 --> 00:45:49,570
model, with a temporary data file.

717
00:45:49,920 --> 00:45:55,554
This one checks parallel calls: three
steps, all in turn one, and the results

718
00:45:55,554 --> 00:45:58,700
matched to the calls
by ID, in one message.

719
00:45:59,300 --> 00:46:05,811
This one checks recovery from invalid
input: first an error step, then a successful

720
00:46:05,811 --> 00:46:08,321
one, and exactly one task saved.

721
00:46:08,921 --> 00:46:14,696
And these check the human approval: when
the answer is no, the task is still there,

722
00:46:14,696 --> 00:46:16,853
and the policy logged declined.

723
00:46:17,203 --> 00:46:19,243
When it's yes, the task is gone.

724
00:46:21,043 --> 00:46:26,999
Forty-three tests, all passing, with no
key, no network, and without touching your

725
00:46:26,999 --> 00:46:28,088
real data file.

726
00:46:28,688 --> 00:46:31,269
And mypy is happy with every type hint.

727
00:46:33,069 --> 00:46:39,004
A four hundred about the tool schema
usually means a strict schema rule: additional

728
00:46:39,004 --> 00:46:42,652
properties must be
false, and every field required.

729
00:46:43,002 --> 00:46:46,762
Generate schemas with
schema for, instead of by hand.

730
00:46:47,362 --> 00:46:51,863
A four hundred about tool use
IDs means a result is missing.

731
00:46:52,213 --> 00:46:56,237
Every call needs its result,
all in the next user message.

732
00:46:56,837 --> 00:47:02,346
If the model picks the wrong tool,
improve the descriptions, and remove tools that

733
00:47:02,346 --> 00:47:02,884
overlap.

734
00:47:03,484 --> 00:47:07,613
And if the agent seems stuck,
a tool probably has no timeout.

735
00:47:07,963 --> 00:47:09,340
Every tool gets one.

736
00:47:11,140 --> 00:47:12,892
Module interview corner.

737
00:47:13,242 --> 00:47:17,619
Question one: walk me through what
happens when an agent calls a tool.

738
00:47:20,219 --> 00:47:23,019
Your code sends the
tools and the messages.

739
00:47:23,369 --> 00:47:28,330
The model replies with a stop
reason of tool use, and tool use blocks.

740
00:47:28,680 --> 00:47:34,680
Your code validates each input, checks
the permission, runs it with a timeout, and

741
00:47:34,680 --> 00:47:40,607
returns the tool results, with the same
IDs, and is error on failures, all in one

742
00:47:40,607 --> 00:47:41,558
user message.

743
00:47:41,908 --> 00:47:44,680
Then repeat until there are no tool calls.

744
00:47:45,030 --> 00:47:50,761
The trap: skipping validation and
permissions, or saying the API runs the tool.

745
00:47:51,361 --> 00:47:56,639
Question two, a scenario: how would
you let an agent send emails safely?

746
00:47:59,239 --> 00:48:01,330
Make send email a danger tool.

747
00:48:01,680 --> 00:48:04,202
Validate the recipients and the content.

748
00:48:04,552 --> 00:48:09,067
Show the human the exact email,
and ask for approval every time.

749
00:48:09,417 --> 00:48:15,424
Log every decision, limit how many it
can send, and start with a draft-only mode.

750
00:48:15,774 --> 00:48:19,289
The trap: adding never
send spam to the system prompt.

751
00:48:19,639 --> 00:48:21,621
Instructions are not enforcement.

752
00:48:22,221 --> 00:48:27,487
And question three: what's the
difference between strict tool use and validating in

753
00:48:27,487 --> 00:48:28,121
your code?

754
00:48:30,721 --> 00:48:36,261
Strict tool use guarantees the input
matches the schema: the fields and the types.

755
00:48:36,611 --> 00:48:42,507
Your validation enforces limits and
business rules, and turns failures into clear

756
00:48:42,507 --> 00:48:44,326
errors the model can fix.

757
00:48:44,676 --> 00:48:47,162
The trap: treating them as alternatives.

758
00:48:47,512 --> 00:48:48,609
You want both.

759
00:48:50,409 --> 00:48:51,715
Module recap.

760
00:48:52,065 --> 00:48:57,698
With tools, the model requests, your
code runs, and the results come back by ID.

761
00:48:58,298 --> 00:49:02,875
A registry and Pydantic give
one source of truth for every tool.

762
00:49:03,475 --> 00:49:08,183
For parallel calls, run them
all, and send one message of results.

763
00:49:08,783 --> 00:49:12,475
Validate every input, and
give every tool a timeout.

764
00:49:13,075 --> 00:49:18,562
And use least privilege, permission
levels, and a human yes for anything dangerous.

765
00:49:20,362 --> 00:49:22,258
Module quiz, question one.

766
00:49:22,608 --> 00:49:24,841
Who actually runs a tool's function?

767
00:49:31,041 --> 00:49:33,466
Your code, after the model requests it.

768
00:49:33,816 --> 00:49:35,376
The model only asks.

769
00:49:37,176 --> 00:49:38,266
Question two.

770
00:49:38,616 --> 00:49:42,038
Why generate tool
schemas from Pydantic classes?

771
00:49:48,238 --> 00:49:53,578
Because one class both describes the
input to the model, and validates it in your

772
00:49:53,578 --> 00:49:53,907
code.

773
00:49:54,257 --> 00:49:55,633
One source of truth.

774
00:49:57,433 --> 00:49:58,564
Question three.

775
00:49:58,914 --> 00:50:02,128
With strict mode on,
which check is still your job?

776
00:50:08,328 --> 00:50:10,974
Checking that the
task with that ID exists.

777
00:50:11,324 --> 00:50:13,190
Strict mode covers the shape.

778
00:50:13,540 --> 00:50:15,270
Business rules are yours.

779
00:50:17,070 --> 00:50:18,184
Question four.

780
00:50:18,534 --> 00:50:21,355
A thread timeout fires after two seconds.

781
00:50:21,705 --> 00:50:23,097
What does it guarantee?

782
00:50:29,297 --> 00:50:32,905
That the agent stops
waiting, and gets an error result.

783
00:50:33,255 --> 00:50:35,170
It doesn't stop the work itself.

784
00:50:36,970 --> 00:50:38,334
And question five.

785
00:50:38,684 --> 00:50:42,087
Where must the human
approval check for delete task live?

786
00:50:48,287 --> 00:50:51,542
In your code, between
validation and execution.

787
00:50:51,892 --> 00:50:53,398
Prompts guide the model.

788
00:50:53,748 --> 00:50:55,324
Only code enforces.

789
00:50:57,124 --> 00:50:59,532
Now some practice, from easy to hard.

790
00:50:59,882 --> 00:51:01,228
Pause and try them.

791
00:51:01,578 --> 00:51:05,140
The answers are in the
module's quiz dot md file.

792
00:51:05,740 --> 00:51:06,379
One.

793
00:51:06,729 --> 00:51:12,407
Write a Pydantic class for a rename
task tool, with a task ID and a new title.

794
00:51:13,007 --> 00:51:13,629
Two.

795
00:51:13,979 --> 00:51:19,084
Which permission level would you give a
tool that clears all completed tasks, and

796
00:51:19,084 --> 00:51:19,336
